{"id":"tiny-clip-capstone","t":"Capstone: train and evaluate a tiny CLIP","tags":["Embeddings","Training"],"d":"Hard","rt":"numpy","fn":"clip_step","tol":[1e-06,1e-08],"pkgs":[],"st":"Train the projection heads of a tiny CLIP for one step and measure whether retrieval improved.\n\nImage features `Xi (n, a)` and text features `Xt (n, b)` come from frozen encoders; row `i` of each describes the same pair. The trainable heads are `Wi (a, d)` and `Wt (b, d)`.\n\nWrite `clip_step(Xi, Xt, Wi, Wt, tau, lr)`:\n\n1. $u = \\text{normalise}(X_i W_i)$, $v = \\text{normalise}(X_t W_t)$ (unit rows). Logits $Z = u v^\\top / \\tau$.\n2. Loss $L = \\tfrac12(\\text{CE}_{rows}(Z) + \\text{CE}_{cols}(Z))$, each averaged over the `n` pairs, with the diagonal as the targets.\n3. Backpropagate through the normalisation: if $z = y / \\lVert y \\rVert$ then $\\partial L/\\partial y = (g - z\\,(z\\cdot g)) / \\lVert y \\rVert$ with $g = \\partial L/\\partial z$.\n4. One SGD step on `Wi` and `Wt`.\n\nReturn `(loss_before, loss_after, r1_before, r1_after)`, where `r1` is image-to-text recall@1: the fraction of rows of $Z$ whose maximum is on the diagonal (ties go to the lowest index, as `argmax` does).","starter":"import numpy as np\n\ndef clip_step(Xi, Xt, Wi, Wt, tau, lr):\n    \"\"\"One SGD step on CLIP projection heads, with loss and recall@1 before and after.\"\"\"\n    raise NotImplementedError\n","hints":["The gradient of the symmetric loss with respect to Z is half of (row softmax minus identity) plus half of (column softmax minus identity), divided by n.","Gradient through normalisation removes the component along z: only changes in direction matter, not in length.","Check the gradient on a tiny case with finite differences before trusting the full step."],"sol":"import numpy as np\n\ndef _norm(Y):\n    r = np.linalg.norm(Y, axis=1, keepdims=True)\n    return Y / r, r\n\ndef _eval(Xi, Xt, Wi, Wt, tau):\n    u, ru = _norm(Xi @ Wi); v, rv = _norm(Xt @ Wt)\n    Z = u @ v.T / tau\n    n = len(Z)\n    Pr = np.exp(Z - Z.max(1, keepdims=True)); Pr /= Pr.sum(1, keepdims=True)\n    Pc = np.exp(Z - Z.max(0, keepdims=True)); Pc /= Pc.sum(0, keepdims=True)\n    d = np.arange(n)\n    loss = float(0.5 * (-np.log(Pr[d, d]).mean() - np.log(Pc[d, d]).mean()))\n    r1 = float((Z.argmax(1) == d).mean())\n    return loss, r1, u, ru, v, rv, Pr, Pc\n\ndef clip_step(Xi, Xt, Wi, Wt, tau, lr):\n    Xi, Xt, Wi, Wt = (np.asarray(a, float) for a in (Xi, Xt, Wi, Wt))\n    loss0, r10, u, ru, v, rv, Pr, Pc = _eval(Xi, Xt, Wi, Wt, tau)\n    n = len(u)\n    dZ = 0.5 * ((Pr - np.eye(n)) + (Pc - np.eye(n))) / n\n    gu = dZ @ v / tau\n    gv = dZ.T @ u / tau\n    dyu = (gu - u * (u * gu).sum(1, keepdims=True)) / ru\n    dyv = (gv - v * (v * gv).sum(1, keepdims=True)) / rv\n    Wi2 = Wi - lr * Xi.T @ dyu\n    Wt2 = Wt - lr * Xt.T @ dyv\n    loss1, r11 = _eval(Xi, Xt, Wi2, Wt2, tau)[:2]\n    return loss0, loss1, r10, r11\n","ex_md":"## What was built\nThe steps gave a scaled similarity matrix, the symmetric contrastive loss, recall@K and zero-shot classification. This capstone joins them: the loss trains the heads, and recall@1 on the same pairs checks that the step helped.\n\n## The gradient\nEvery image competes against every caption and every caption against every image, so $\\partial L/\\partial Z$ is a dense $n \\times n$ matrix. Each pair pushes its own entry up and pulls the others down, weighted by how much probability they currently take. That is why CLIP needs large batches: more negatives give a sharper signal.\n\n## Normalisation\nCLIP compares directions only. The projection through $(I - zz^\\top)/\\lVert y\\rVert$ removes any change in length, so the heads cannot reduce the loss by growing their outputs. The temperature does that job instead, and real CLIP learns $\\log(1/\\tau)$ with a cap at 100.\n\n## The last test\nThe text head starts with its axes swapped, so recall@1 is 0. A single step is not enough to rotate it the right way, which is a reminder that a falling loss can precede any change in the ranking metric.\n\n## Interview follow-ups\n- Recall@1 measured on the training pairs is optimistic. How would you set up a held-out retrieval evaluation, and what makes it hard to keep the test captions out of a web-scale training set?","nex":2,"shown":[{"in":"array([[ 1.24035481, -2.05059973, -0.65050229, -0.76049473, -0.51886855],\n       [ 2.07599766, -1.98969372, -1.14369601, -0.50042281, -2.25876756],\n       [ 0.79933686, -1.38765681, -0.30136131, -0.34831356, -1.2297461 ],\n       [ 0.0935545 ,  0.2334569 ,  1.20280933, -0.93945645,  0.38168075],\n       [ 0.15840086,  0.7900587 , -1.04084448,  1.04190785, -0.70820794],\n       [-0.86535971, -0.41943879, -0.67330523,  0.23506931,  0.57395162]])\narray([[ 0.41179542, -1.65718827, -0.14826763,  1.13832408],\n       [ 1.86415979, -1.88215921,  1.23243228,  0.42571779],\n       [ 0.56895909, -1.09577165,  0.8766291 ,  0.48553562],\n       [-0.46710358, -0.29776873,  0.42172682,  0.50551223],\n       [ 0.91218525,  0.48407989, -0.13802839, -0.17841486],\n       [-1.22121899,  0.24955873, -0.47950516, -0.31204963]])\narray([[ 0.14883877, -0.29126172,  0.38569704],\n       [-0.05616649,  0.29925443,  0.54508343],\n       [ 0.17125132, -0.3916344 , -0.67722388],\n       [ 0.78413722, -0.04977138, -0.30793561],\n       [ 0.06451373, -0.08560175,  0.38108961]])\narray([[ 0.01696409,  0.00687479, -0.35728986],\n       [ 0.23478405, -0.51693336,  0.33294472],\n       [ 0.76196876, -0.76234302, -1.23311462],\n       [ 0.30843938,  1.27394891, -0.50046242]])\n0.5\n0.5","out":"(2.415440867990694, 1.0715786303904045, 0.0, 0.5)"},{"in":"array([[-0.79017581, -0.60167028,  0.12278311, -0.22236655, -0.60787801,\n        -0.77977114],\n       [ 3.22611823, -0.34824086, -4.06279457,  4.39209193,  4.92530624,\n         6.61103392],\n       [ 0.95868726,  0.38886675,  0.55990831,  1.05575685,  0.70993414,\n         0.98476021],\n       [ 1.62950303, -0.63664031,  0.21232026,  1.71306487,  1.283